NOVA bundles and fetches data from other open-source projects.

sherlock-project/sherlock (MIT License)
  The username site database follows Sherlock's data.json schema, and
  `--refresh-sites` downloads their maintained catalogue directly. The curated
  list in nova_osint/data/sites.json is derived from that file. Sherlock's
  daily false-positive exclusion list is fetched at runtime and honoured.
  https://github.com/sherlock-project/sherlock

soxoj/maigret (MIT License, Copyright 2020-2026 Soxoj)
  Maigret's site database is fetched at runtime (never bundled). Its ranked,
  enabled sites widen the username catalogue, and its presence/absence/error
  strings replace Sherlock's check where both projects know a site.
  https://github.com/soxoj/maigret

disposable-email-domains/disposable-email-domains (MIT License)
  The throwaway-mail blocklist is fetched from this project at runtime and
  merged with the small seed list in nova_osint/data/disposable.txt.
  https://github.com/disposable-email-domains/disposable-email-domains

Public services queried at runtime, each under its own terms:
  rdap.org, Cloudflare DNS (including its malware-filtering resolver),
  AdGuard DNS, Google Public DNS, crt.sh, SSLMate CertSpotter, crt.name,
  Shodan CT and InternetDB, ip.thc.org, AnubisDB, HackerTarget, RapidDNS,
  urlscan.io, Have I Been Pwned, XposedOrNot, keys.openpgp.org, ip-api.com,
  the Internet Archive, Gravatar and the GitHub REST API.

Methods learned from (ideas only, no code copied): SpiderFoot (MIT),
WhatsMyName (CC BY-SA 4.0; its data is not used), theHarvester (GPL-2.0),
OWASP Amass (Apache-2.0), subfinder (MIT), Holehe (GPL-3.0), PhoneInfoga
(GPL-3.0) and OpenOSINT (MIT).
